  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Figshare dataset is an openly available research resource supporting a 2025 study on G protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolution in the closest living relatives of metazoa. The record already shows early scholarly uptake, with 1 citation and 2 documented downstream reuses, indicating that the files are being used beyond simple repository deposit.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset collects the GPCR sequences, HMMs, seed alignments, and phylogenetic analyses underpinning the associated study, making it a reusable methods-and-data package rather than a standalone reference item. Its open access status and repository deposit in Figshare support straightforward reuse by other researchers working on phylogenetics, evolutionary biology, and receptor diversity.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Impact evidence is still early, but it is concrete. OpenAlex records 1 citation, and Crossref data-citation metadata shows the dataset being cited as supplemental material in the associated eLife article. The reuse graph also captures 2 downstream reuses, including the journal article itself and a Europe PMC article mention, which together suggest that the dataset is already functioning as part of the scholarly record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is narrow so far, with the single citing work appearing in eLife and centered on biology-adjacent themes such as marine and coastal ecosystems, protist diversity and phylogeny, and fish biology, ecology, and behavior. That pattern is consistent with a specialized dataset being taken up within a focused research community rather than broadly across disciplines.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the wider attention record is quiet: there are no recorded online mentions in the available event data, and DataCite reports no views or downloads in this snapshot. Taken together, the evidence points to an early-stage but credible reuse trajectory, with the clearest signal coming from scholarly citation and dataset supplementation rather than public discussion.</p>",
